sections:

#### prop_section ####
kind: proposition
----
statement: To find the first binomial line.
----
proof:

<div class="just"><a href="/elements/books/bookX/propositions/propX28/#lemma1">X.28,Lemma1</a></div>

Set out two numbers {AC} and {CB} such that the sum of them {AB} has to {BC|CB} the ratio which a square number has to a square number, but does not have to {CA|AC} the ratio which a square number has to a square number.

Set out any rational straight line {D}, and let {EF} be commensurable in length with {D}. Therefore {EF} is also rational.

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="250" height="130" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="X.48 diagram" src="propX48.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    // Joyce's figure: the rational line D, the binomial EG divided at F, the
    // line H (with the squares on FG and H summing to the square on EF), and
    // the numbers AC, CB laid out on AB. Added, all invisible at rest:
    // transparent highlight targets AC, CB (;0;0;0); initiallyHidden solid
    // twins EF and FG, so EF stands when it is set out and FG strokes from
    // F when it is contrived, before Joyce's EG appears with "EG is
    // binomial". Captions reach Joyce's spellings with the display override
    // ({BA|AB}, {BC|CB}, {CA|AC}). Joyce's closing "Therefore EF is a first
